FEED your model
A way to think about it is we are going to take some set of [data] and feed or push it through our model to get some [output]
Example: [0 and 1s] –&gt;
[complex model with lots of covariates but the focal parameter is a treatment vs control] –&gt;
[estimated means for treatment and control groups]

Level 2
Level 2 takes the parameters at level 1 and decomposes them into a fixed component ( \(\gamma\) ) that reflects the average and, if desired, the individual deviations around that fixed effect (U).
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Level 1:}\quad Y_{ij} &= \beta_{0i} + \beta_{1i}X_{ij} + \varepsilon_{ij} \\
\text{Level 2:}\quad \beta_{0i} &= \gamma_{00} + U_{0i} \\
\beta_{1i} &= \gamma_{10}
\end{aligned}
\]

Adding a random slope
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Level 1:}\quad Y_{ij} &= \beta_{0i} + \beta_{1i}X_{ij} + \varepsilon_{ij} \\
\text{Level 2:}\quad \beta_{0i} &= \gamma_{00} + U_{0i} \\
\beta_{1i} &= \gamma_{10} + U_{1i} \\
\text{Combined:}\quad Y_{ij} &= \gamma_{00} + \gamma_{10}X_{ij} + U_{0i} + U_{1i}X_{ij} + \varepsilon_{ij}
\end{aligned}
\]
Each person’s slope is a fixed part plus their own deviation: for person 16, \(\beta_{1,16} = \gamma_{10} + U_{1,16}\).

Level 2 predictors
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Level 1:}\quad Y_{ij} &= \beta_{0i} + \beta_{1i}X_{ij} + \varepsilon_{ij} \\
\text{Level 2:}\quad \beta_{0i} &= \gamma_{00} + \gamma_{01}G_{i} + U_{0i} \\
\beta_{1i} &= \gamma_{10} + U_{1i}
\end{aligned}
\]
\(G_i\) is a person-level variable (it has no j): it can only predict person-level things, like each person’s intercept.

Cross-level interactions
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Level 1:}\quad Y_{ij} &= \beta_{0i} + \beta_{1i}X_{ij} + \varepsilon_{ij} \\
\text{Level 2:}\quad \beta_{0i} &= \gamma_{00} + \gamma_{01}G_{i} + U_{0i} \\
\beta_{1i} &= \gamma_{10} + \gamma_{11}G_{i} + U_{1i} \\
\text{Combined:}\quad Y_{ij} &= \gamma_{00} + \gamma_{01}G_{i} + \gamma_{10}X_{ij} + \gamma_{11}G_{i}X_{ij} + U_{0i} + U_{1i}X_{ij} + \varepsilon_{ij}
\end{aligned}
\]
\(\gamma_{11}\), the cross-level interaction: does the person-level variable G predict how strong each person’s X effect is?

Logistic regression in Stan
The mortality model from the GLM lecture (glm.4), written by hand:

logit_code &lt;- "
data {
  int&lt;lower=1&gt; N;                       // number of people
  vector[N] iq;                         // centered IQ
  array[N] int&lt;lower=0, upper=1&gt; y;     // 1 = not a premature death
  real iq_new;                          // an IQ value to predict at
}
parameters {
  real alpha;                           // log odds at average IQ
  real beta;                            // change in log odds per IQ point
}
model {
  alpha ~ normal(0, 2);                 // priors
  beta ~ normal(0, 0.5);
  y ~ bernoulli_logit(alpha + beta * iq);  // likelihood
}
generated quantities {
  real p_new = inv_logit(alpha + beta * iq_new);
  real odds_ratio = exp(beta);
}
"

~ vs target +=
These two lines add the same thing to the log posterior (except constants that don’t matter for sampling):
y ~ bernoulli_logit(alpha + beta * iq);
target += bernoulli_logit_lpmf(y | alpha + beta * iq);
brms writes the target += form. Think of the model block as adding up log prior + log likelihood, one line at a time.

Q2. How much does the prior matter?
Refit your HW 2 model with a deliberately bad prior on the slope: strong and centered in the wrong place. For example, if your slope is positive, use something like normal(-10, 1) scaled to your variables. Then:

Plot the slope’s posterior from HW 2 and from this new model on the same plot.
Did the bad prior move the posterior much? Using your sample size and the prior’s width, explain why or why not. What would have to be true about a study for the prior to really matter?
Sequential updating in brms. In HW 1 you saw that “today’s posterior is tomorrow’s prior” works exactly on a grid. Try it with your HW 2 model:

randomly split your data into two halves (set.seed() first);
fit your HW 2 model (with its original priors) to the first half;
turn the posterior for the intercept and slope into normal priors (posterior mean and SD) and fit the second half with them;
compare the slope from this two-step fit with the slope from fitting all the data at once.

How close are they? Why is the brms version only approximately the same, when the grid version in HW 1 was exact? (Hint: what information about the first-half posterior did you throw away by summarizing it with a mean and an SD? What did you do about σ?)

